          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "**Urgency: routine, based on what you've described.** The urgency check found no emergency pattern in your description. That is not a clearance. It only means nothing you wrote matched its list. You did not mention breathlessness, chest pain, coughing blood, or weight loss, so I can't tell whether any of them apply to you.\n\n**Check these now. If any apply, get urgent medical help, not a routine appointment:**\n- Breathlessness that is getting worse, or breathlessness at rest. Confusion or unusual drowsiness can also be serious when someone is struggling to breathe. (Upper respiratory infection and COPD documents)\n- Chest pain. Chest discomfort should not be assumed to be heartburn. Pain brought on by exertion, or pain with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or spreading to the arm, neck or jaw, needs emergency assessment. (GERD document)\n- Coughing up blood. (COPD document)\n- Blue lips or fingers. (COPD document)\n- A fever with a marked change in mucus, or a fever returning after you had started to improve. The influenza document notes that secondary bacterial pneumonia often appears as a worsening after several days of improvement.\n- Unplanned weight loss, or trouble or pain when swallowing, vomiting blood, or black tarry stools. These need prompt assessment. (GERD document)\n\n**What the reference material says about your situation:** A cough that lasts two to three weeks after a cold is described as normal and is not by itself a sign of a bacterial infection. Yellow or green mucus is also part of the usual course. (Upper respiratory infection and antibiotic stewardship documents) Your symptoms span several body systems. The main groups to consider are:\n- **Respiratory:** a lingering post-viral cough, or conditions such as asthma (which can cause a cough alone, especially at night) and COPD. (Asthma and COPD documents)\n- **Cardiovascular:** only relevant if you have chest pain. Any chest pain should be checked for a heart cause first.\n- **Digestive:** reflux can cause a persistent cough. (GERD document)\n\nThese are areas to raise with a clinician, not a diagnosis. The reference material I could access does not say which one, if any, applies to you.\n\n**Next step:** Book a routine appointment if your cough has lasted more than about three weeks, or if it is not improving. Seek same-day care if anything in the list above applies. If you are struggling to breathe or have chest pain now, call emergency services.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
